# What never enters a build context.
#
# `node_modules` is first and is the whole reason this file exists. On Windows it holds
# `@pyquest/*` as symlinks to absolute Docker-VM paths, which resolve on the host and nowhere
# else — copying it into an image would carry the exact bug the Dockerfiles are here to fix, and
# would do it silently, because the symlinks look like directories until something imports one.
#
# Every image runs `npm ci` for itself. The host's install is not an input to any build.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output. Each image builds what it needs; a stale `dist` from the host would be a second,
# older answer sitting beside the fresh one.
dist
**/dist

# `**/` matters here and its absence cost a build.
#
# A bare `*.tsbuildinfo` matches the context root only, so `packages/db/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo` was
# copied in from the host — and `tsc -b` read it, concluded the project was already built, and
# emitted nothing. The image then had `src/` and no `dist/`, and the container died on
# `Cannot find module '/workspace/packages/db/dist/cli.js'`.
#
# An incremental build cache from another machine is the most misleading thing that can enter a
# build context: it does not fail, it succeeds at doing nothing.
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Python bytecode from the curriculum's own verifiers, which has no business in a node image.
__pycache__
**/__pycache__

# Local environment. Secrets reach a container through compose, never through a layer — a value
# baked into an image is a value that leaks with the image.
.env
.env.*

# Noise: it slows the build and none of it is read at run time.
.git
.vscode
coverage
*.log
